    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `triage` in `app/worker.py`. The existing tests pass (`2 passed`), and it returned the right result when I ran it against a fake API response.\n\n**How it works:** it's the async version of `ask_with_limits`, making one call through `client.messages.with_raw_response.create(...)` with `MODEL`, `max_tokens=512`, `SYSTEM`, and the ticket as the only user message. The answer and `tokens_remaining` both come from that same response, so each ticket costs one request. It returns `answer`, `tokens_remaining`, `request_id`, and `throttle = tokens_remaining < LOW_WATERMARK`.\n\n**One difference from the sync version:** in the installed `anthropic==1.8.0`, `parse()` on the async client has to be awaited (`await raw.parse()`). Header access stays a normal attribute lookup. I confirmed this with Context7 and in the installed SDK source.\n\n**Testing:** the existing tests don't call `triage`, so I ran it against a fake API response with 1500 tokens remaining. It returned `{'answer': 'billing: charged twice', 'tokens_remaining': 1500, 'request_id': 'req_1', 'throttle': True}` and sent exactly one request with the expected model, system prompt, `max_tokens` and message. I didn't add a test for this to the suite.\n\n**One thing to clean up:** my temporary script `_chk.py` is still in the project root because the delete command needed your approval. It's safe to remove it with `rm _chk.py`."
